1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,393
Two athletes step onto the mat.

2
00:00:01,693 --> 00:00:03,551
Same height, same weight, same training.

3
00:00:03,851 --> 00:00:06,312
They fight, and it's close, the kind that could go either way.

4
00:00:06,612 --> 00:00:07,634
And then one of them wins.

5
00:00:08,134 --> 00:00:11,385
Now watch what happens if we rewind, swap the color of their gear, and run it back.

6
00:00:11,685 --> 00:00:12,799
The winner might change.

7
00:00:13,299 --> 00:00:14,925
Not the fighting, just the color.

8
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:18,072
Same two people, same moves, and a different hand gets raised.

9
00:00:18,372 --> 00:00:20,647
I first ran into this on a test, of all places.

10
00:00:20,947 --> 00:00:24,384
A reading passage claimed the color you wear can change who wins, and who loses.

11
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:25,845
It sounded like nonsense.

12
00:00:26,345 --> 00:00:26,948
It isn't.

13
00:00:27,348 --> 00:00:28,881
And it goes a lot deeper than sports.

14
00:00:28,881 --> 00:00:31,017
So why would a color do anything at all?

15
00:00:31,517 --> 00:00:33,700
Because in nature, red is rarely a decoration.

16
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:34,975
It's a status report.

17
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:39,237
A male mandrill's face turns a brighter red as he climbs the ranks, and fades when he falls.

18
00:00:39,537 --> 00:00:42,137
That color is wired straight to his testosterone, and he can't fake it.

19
00:00:42,637 --> 00:00:46,167
Small songbirds size each other up by the red of their beaks, and the redder bird usually wins.

20
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:49,578
Across primates, birds, and fish, red keeps meaning the same thing.

21
00:00:50,078 --> 00:00:50,775
Back off.

22
00:00:51,175 --> 00:00:53,265
So the real question isn't why animals react to red.

23
00:00:53,565 --> 00:00:54,865
It's whether we ever stopped.

24
00:00:54,865 --> 00:00:57,048
In 2004, two researchers went looking.

25
00:00:57,348 --> 00:01:00,273
They pulled the results from four combat sports at the Athens Olympics.

26
00:01:00,573 --> 00:01:02,942
Boxing, taekwondo, and two kinds of wrestling.

27
00:01:03,242 --> 00:01:06,493
And here's the crucial part, in these sports, the gear is handed out at random.

28
00:01:06,793 --> 00:01:08,418
Red or blue, nobody picks.

29
00:01:08,718 --> 00:01:09,647
It's a coin flip.

30
00:01:10,147 --> 00:01:12,608
So if color did nothing, red should win half the time.

31
00:01:12,908 --> 00:01:13,465
It didn't.

32
00:01:13,865 --> 00:01:15,398
Red won fifty-five percent of bouts.

33
00:01:15,898 --> 00:01:19,288
And in the closest fights, the ones that could tip either way, red won sixty-two.

34
00:01:19,788 --> 00:01:22,667
A coin that lands red sixty-two percent of the time isn't a fair coin.

35
00:01:23,167 --> 00:01:24,235
Something was loading it.

36
00:01:24,235 --> 00:01:25,768
So there were two suspects.

37
00:01:26,268 --> 00:01:27,197
Suspect one.

38
00:01:27,697 --> 00:01:29,322
The color changes the fighters themselves.

39
00:01:29,622 --> 00:01:32,501
Maybe red makes you feel more dangerous, and makes your opponent flinch.

40
00:01:32,801 --> 00:01:33,637
Suspect two.

41
00:01:34,137 --> 00:01:35,856
The color changes the person keeping score.

42
00:01:36,156 --> 00:01:39,081
Either way, the fighters are identical. The referee is the one who's biased.

43
00:01:39,581 --> 00:01:42,925
In a real match, both could be true at once, and you would never tell them apart.

44
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:46,811
You would need a way to freeze the fighters, and test only the judge.

45
00:01:46,811 --> 00:01:48,251
Which is exactly what someone did.

46
00:01:48,551 --> 00:01:52,080
They took real taekwondo footage and showed it to forty-two experienced referees to score.

47
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:55,120
Then they took the same clips and digitally swapped the colors.

48
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,417
The fighter who was in red was now in blue.

49
00:01:57,717 --> 00:01:59,807
Same kick, same human, same exact moment.

50
00:02:00,307 --> 00:02:02,211
The only thing that changed was a color filter.

51
00:02:02,511 --> 00:02:04,694
The red athletes scored about thirteen percent higher.

52
00:02:05,194 --> 00:02:07,469
Thirteen percent more points, just for being wrapped in red.

53
00:02:07,969 --> 00:02:09,270
The bias wasn't on the mat.

54
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:13,199
It was behind the scoring table, in the eyes of people who'd have sworn they were being fair.

55
00:02:13,199 --> 00:02:15,660
And once you know to look for it, you start seeing it everywhere.

56
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:19,525
Years earlier, researchers dug through penalty records in pro football and hockey.

57
00:02:19,825 --> 00:02:23,355
Teams that wore black got penalized more, not occasionally, but consistently.

58
00:02:23,855 --> 00:02:28,035
Two teams even changed their jerseys to black one season, and their penalties jumped right after.

59
00:02:28,335 --> 00:02:30,564
Now, maybe players in black really do play dirtier.

60
00:02:30,864 --> 00:02:34,114
So the researchers filmed the exact same plays, once in white, once in black.

61
00:02:34,414 --> 00:02:36,272
Referees called the black version more aggressive.

62
00:02:36,772 --> 00:02:39,187
Red flatters you to the judge, and black incriminates you.

63
00:02:39,487 --> 00:02:41,252
Either way, they're not scoring what you did.

64
00:02:41,552 --> 00:02:42,945
They're scoring what you wore.

65
00:02:42,945 --> 00:02:44,802
Now take it somewhere the score actually matters.

66
00:02:45,602 --> 00:02:48,575
A courtroom is just another room where people watch someone and decide a number.

67
00:02:48,875 --> 00:02:50,593
Except here, the number is years.

68
00:02:51,093 --> 00:02:53,090
And juries aren't immune to the packaging either.

69
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:57,291
Defendants who look the part get judged more dangerous, and sentenced harder, for the same crime.

70
00:02:57,791 --> 00:02:59,045
Lawyers have known this forever.

71
00:02:59,345 --> 00:03:02,828
It's why they put their clients in soft grays and quiet collars, never red.

72
00:03:03,128 --> 00:03:04,474
Now, be honest with yourself here.

73
00:03:04,854 --> 00:03:09,080
This is softer science than the sports studies, mostly mock juries, and more about clothes than color.

74
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:11,563
But it's the same machine we just watched mis-score a kick.

75
00:03:11,863 --> 00:03:13,628
And it doesn't switch off when the stakes go up.

76
00:03:14,128 --> 00:03:15,800
If anything, it just hides better.

77
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,889
And then there's the one everybody wants to be true.

78
00:03:18,269 --> 00:03:23,238
Around 2008, studies claimed a woman in red looked more attractive to men, and the men had no idea it was happening.

79
00:03:23,538 --> 00:03:24,467
It's a great story.

80
00:03:24,767 --> 00:03:28,111
But here's the catch, when other scientists tried to repeat it, it mostly fell apart.

81
00:03:28,611 --> 00:03:31,769
So I'm not going to promise you that red fixes your dating life.

82
00:03:32,069 --> 00:03:33,926
Honestly, nobody's even sure this one is real.

83
00:03:34,426 --> 00:03:35,541
And that's kind of the point.

84
00:03:35,841 --> 00:03:40,263
A good color story is so tempting that even the scientists chasing it can get fooled.

85
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:43,003
So where does that leave the red advantage, the original, solid one?

86
00:03:43,503 --> 00:03:44,850
You'd expect it to still be there.

87
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:46,822
For twenty years, everyone assumed it was.

88
00:03:47,122 --> 00:03:49,722
Then someone went back and checked, with two decades of new data.

89
00:03:50,022 --> 00:03:51,230
And the advantage was gone.

90
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:54,961
Today, red wins about fifty point five percent, a coin flip again.

91
00:03:55,461 --> 00:03:56,204
So what changed?

92
00:03:56,704 --> 00:03:57,355
The sport did.

93
00:03:57,855 --> 00:04:00,084
Taekwondo brought in electronic scoring and instant replay.

94
00:04:00,384 --> 00:04:03,635
The human referee got quietly demoted, and a machine started counting the hits.

95
00:04:03,935 --> 00:04:06,767
And the moment the biased judge stepped back, the magic evaporated.

96
00:04:07,267 --> 00:04:08,103
Think about that.

97
00:04:08,483 --> 00:04:10,898
If red had been making the fighters better, it should have survived.

98
00:04:11,198 --> 00:04:12,313
The fighters are still human.

99
00:04:12,613 --> 00:04:14,749
It died the instant we stopped trusting a person to score.

100
00:04:15,249 --> 00:04:17,478
Which tells you the advantage was never really in the red.

101
00:04:18,098 --> 00:04:18,934
It was in us.

102
00:04:18,934 --> 00:04:21,256
We'd like to believe we judge what's actually in front of us.

103
00:04:21,556 --> 00:04:23,181
The kick, the play, the person.

104
00:04:23,681 --> 00:04:26,746
But mostly, we judge the wrapping, and then invent a reason that sounds like merit.

105
00:04:27,246 --> 00:04:28,825
The fix is almost insultingly simple.

106
00:04:29,125 --> 00:04:32,005
Take the human out of the scoring, and the bias leaves with them.

107
00:04:32,305 --> 00:04:34,162
But most of life doesn't have instant replay.

108
00:04:34,662 --> 00:04:39,585
Most of the time, it's just you, a color you barely chose, and someone across the table who's certain they're being fair.

109
00:04:40,085 --> 00:04:41,478
So here's the thing worth chewing on.

110
00:04:41,858 --> 00:04:46,688
If a single color can quietly bend a trained referee, what else are you being scored on that you've never once noticed?

111
00:04:46,688 --> 00:04:49,056
If you had fun chasing that one down, good news.

112
00:04:49,436 --> 00:04:52,548
That's pretty much the whole channel, one strange, true thing at a time.

113
00:04:52,968 --> 00:04:54,825
So stick around, and come back for the next one.

114
00:04:55,245 --> 00:04:58,775
And if this made you look twice at something in your own life, do me a favor and hit subscribe.

115
00:04:59,195 --> 00:05:01,656
It genuinely helps a channel like this one more than you'd think.

